course: dictionary
lesson: 10582
title: Layers and clues: undercoat
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is meant. Undercoat is a good example: it can be a layer of paint or a layer of fur. «Put on an undercoat, then the topcoat» is about painting, because the topcoat is paint too.
explain: The prefix under- is a clue. Under means below, and coat means a layer covering something. So an undercoat is the layer that goes underneath, before the top layer.
explain: Look at the other words in the sentence. «Yaks live on the Tibetan plateau, with long thick hair, a woolly undercoat and big lungs» lists parts of an animal's body and coat, so the undercoat must be a layer of fur. Woolly is a clue that it is soft.
fact: An undercoat is a first layer. It goes underneath the top layer.
fact: In paint, an undercoat helps the top coat stick and look smooth.
fact: In animals, the undercoat is the soft, thick fur close to the skin, under the longer outer hairs.
fact: «Woolly mammoths» lived during the last Ice Age, and each had «a woolly undercoat» under its longer hairs.
fact: Undercoat can also be a verb, but it is rare: to undercoat something means to give it a first layer of paint.
exercise: «Put on an undercoat, then the topcoat.» In this sentence, what is an undercoat? | *A first layer of paint, put on before the top layer | A bag for carrying paint | A hairy layer on an animal | A tool for cutting wood
exercise: «Yaks live on the Tibetan plateau, with long thick hair, a woolly undercoat and big lungs.» Which word suggests the undercoat is soft and warm? | plateau | *woolly | Tibetan | lungs
exercise: «Woolly mammoths had a woolly undercoat, long guard hairs and a thick layer of fat.» What does undercoat mean here? | The long hairs that stick out | Paint put on a wall | *The soft, thick layer of fur close to the skin, under the longer hairs | A thick layer of fat under the skin
test: In «a woolly undercoat, long guard hairs», which layer is closer to the skin? | The guard hairs | *The undercoat | Both are the same distance | The fat
test: When painting a wall, which comes first? | *Undercoat first, then topcoat | They go on at the same time | Topcoat first, then undercoat | Neither is needed
exam: Why does the prefix under- help you guess what undercoat means? | *Under means below, so an undercoat is a layer below the top one | Under means fast, so an undercoat is quick to put on | Under means very old, so an undercoat is old paint | Under means wet, so an undercoat is always damp
